<h2 id="frequently-asked-questions">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<h3 id="how-many-of-the-eight-o-1-criteria-do-i-need-to-meet">How many of the eight O-1 criteria do I need to meet?</h3>
<p>At least three. USCIS requires documentation in three different categories from the list, or comparable evidence where the criteria do not fit. Three is the floor, not a guarantee. Petitions reaching four or five categories hold up better than ones barely clearing three.</p>
<h3 id="can-i-petition-for-an-o-1-visa-myself">Can I petition for an O-1 visa myself?</h3>
<p>No. Unlike the EB-1A green card, the O-1 is filed by a U.S. employer, a U.S. agent, or a foreign employer through a U.S. agent using Form I-129. Freelancers and independent artists often work through an agent who files on their behalf.</p>
<h3 id="how-long-does-an-o-1-visa-last">How long does an O-1 visa last?</h3>
<p>Up to three years initially. Extensions come in increments of up to one year, tied to completing the same event or activity described in the original petition. There is no fixed lifetime limit, but each extension has to be justified.</p>
<h3 id="is-there-a-cap-or-lottery-for-the-o-1">Is there a cap or lottery for the O-1?</h3>
<p>No. The O-1 has no annual numerical limit and no registration lottery. That is why it attracts people whose employers missed the H-1B filing window, or whose case was not selected in the cap process. The constraint on an O-1 is evidence and timing, not a lottery you have to win.</p>
<h3 id="can-i-change-jobs-while-on-an-o-1">Can I change jobs while on an O-1?</h3>
<p>You can, but the new employer must file a new Form I-129 with evidence of the new employment. If an agent filed your original petition, the new employer files an amended petition. Do not start the new role before the petition is approved.</p>
<h3 id="do-i-need-a-college-degree-for-an-o-1">Do I need a college degree for an O-1?</h3>
<p>No. The O-1 has no degree requirement. That separates it from the H-1B, which requires a specialty occupation and a bachelor's degree or equivalent. For the O-1, the question is your record, not your transcript. What matters instead is third-party proof of standing: awards, press, judging invitations, and letters from people who know the field.</p>
